  "result": "PPO is a reinforcement learning method. The model tries things, gets a score, and gets nudged toward what scored well, with a limit on how far it can move in one step so training stays stable.\n\nYou don't need it for the makerspace assistant. Training models is out of scope in your mission, and you'd need lots of example conversations, a scoring setup and GPUs, which your small budget can't cover. A ready-made model with good tool descriptions gets you most of the way.\n\nWhat usually fixes tool-calling in practice is the tool description. Say a student asks \"Can I use the laser cutter Friday at 2?\" and the assistant calls `search_manuals` when it should call `check_calendar`. That's almost always because both descriptions are vague. Rewriting `check_calendar` as \"Returns open time slots for one machine on one date. Use for any question about availability\" often fixes it.\n\nIf you still want the derivation later, you'd first need basic probability, gradient descent and how a model produces its output probabilities. I can set that up as a separate lesson after your design review.\n\nWant to pick one of your planned tools and draft its description together?",
